WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.946
Before Chang'e 4 could land on the far side of the Moon, China had to solve a basic communication problem.

00:00:06.946 --> 00:00:09.764
The far side of the Moon never faces Earth.

00:00:09.764 --> 00:00:15.400
A spacecraft on the surface there has no direct radio path to anywhere on this planet.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:17.366
The Moon itself is in the way.

00:00:17.366 --> 00:00:19.856
The solution was launched in May 2018.

00:00:19.856 --> 00:00:25.622
A relay satellite called Queqiao — pronounced cheh-chee-ow — Chinese for magpie bridge .

00:00:25.622 --> 00:00:32.438
Queqiao orbits the Earth-Moon L2 Lagrange point — a different L2 from the Sun-Earth L2 where JWST lives.

00:00:32.438 --> 00:00:38.204
This one sits sixty-five thousand kilometers beyond the Moon, along the Earth-Moon line.

00:00:38.204 --> 00:00:44.692
From that position, Queqiao has line-of-sight to both Earth and the Moon's far side simultaneously.

00:00:44.692 --> 00:00:52.031
It is the relay that forwards every signal from Chang'e 4 and the Yutu-2 rover back to ground stations on Earth.

00:00:52.031 --> 00:00:54.325
Why is it called the magpie bridge?

00:00:54.325 --> 00:01:08.217
In Chinese folklore, there are two lovers — a weaver girl and a cowherd — separated permanently by the Milky Way, allowed to meet only once a year, on the seventh night of the seventh month of the lunar calendar.

00:01:08.217 --> 00:01:16.998
When that night comes, all the world's magpies fly up and form a bridge of feathers across the river of stars so the lovers can cross.

00:01:16.998 --> 00:01:20.406
The lovers are the orbits of the Earth and the Moon.

00:01:20.406 --> 00:01:21.847
Queqiao is the bridge.

00:01:21.847 --> 00:01:25.255
Queqiao has been operating for over seven years now.

00:01:25.255 --> 00:01:36.723
Yutu-2 has been driving around the floor of the Von Kármán crater that whole time — over a kilometer of slow exploration, all of it transmitted home through the magpie bridge.

00:01:36.723 --> 00:01:45.569
Chang'e 6 — which returned the first samples from the far side in 2024 — used Queqiao 2, a successor satellite, in the same halo orbit.

00:01:45.569 --> 00:01:51.991
Every photograph the world has ever seen from the far side of the Moon arrived through this relay.

00:01:51.991 --> 00:01:58.872
Without it, the far side stays exactly as silent as it was in 1959 — before Luna 3 first photographed it.

